The preceding implementation is correct under the guaranteed single-pair contract. Three edge-case checks:

• Duplicate values: nums = [3, 3], target = 6. At i = 0, complement 3 is absent, so store 3 → 0. At i = 1, complement 3 exists; return [0, 1], using distinct indices.
• Most likely to expose accidental index reuse: nums = [3, 2, 4], target = 6. At i = 0, complement 3 is absent; store 3 → 0, rather than returning [0, 0]. At i = 1, complement 4 is absent; store 2 → 1. At i = 2, complement 2 exists; return [1, 2].
• Zero and negative numbers: nums = [0, -2, 2], target = 0. At i = 0, complement 0 is absent; store 0 → 0. At i = 1, complement 2 is absent; store -2 → 1. At i = 2, complement -2 exists; return [1, 2].

Each case has exactly one valid unordered pair. The map contains only earlier indices, so checking before insertion prevents self-pairing. No correction is needed. Expected time: O(n); space: O(n).

